# Flashcard deck for guide.html. Each entry: (session, term, definition).
CARDS = [
    (1, "Entrepreneurial Thought and Action (ET&A)", "A way to choose how to act under uncertainty by combining prediction logic with creation (effectual) logic, called CreAction in the course."),
    (1, "Prediction (causal) logic", "Best when the future is knowable: plan first, focus on expected return, secure resources first, follow a chosen path, avoid failure."),
    (1, "Creation (effectual) logic", "Best under uncertainty: act in small steps, limit acceptable loss, start with means at hand, adjust as evidence changes, use intelligent failure as information."),
    (1, "CreAction sequence", "1) Start with who you are, what you know, whom you know. 2) Take a small affordable action. 3) Observe results. 4) Bring in people or resources. 5) Adjust."),
    (1, "Acceptable loss", "Size each step by what you can afford to lose, not by expected return."),
    (2, "Organizational culture", "The shared values, beliefs, assumptions, and norms that show how work is really done, visible in repeated behavior, stories, rituals, rewards, and what leaders tolerate."),
    (2, "Rule vs. norm", "A rule is explicit and usually written. A norm is an informal expectation formed by repeated, reinforced behavior. When they conflict, people follow what the group rewards."),
    (2, "Visible vs. invisible culture", "Visible: schedules, dress, language, rituals, workspace, policies. Invisible: assumptions about status, conflict, effort, trust, risk, and who can influence decisions."),
    (2, "Team charter", "Turns aspirations into behavior: purpose, roles, decision methods, meetings, communication, conflict, accountability, and how the team revises its process."),
    (3, "Idea vs. opportunity", "An idea is an early insight. An opportunity connects a defined customer and meaningful unmet need with a solution that can be delivered and sustained."),
    (3, "Opportunity test", "Attractive (customers act), durable (need and advantage last), timely (good moment), value-creating (benefit the customer values)."),
    (3, "Problem statement template", "As a [specific person in a context], I need a way to [make progress] without [important obstacle or tradeoff]."),
    (3, "Brainstorming rules", "Go for quantity, stay on topic, be visual, build on ideas, one conversation at a time, defer judgment, encourage wild ideas."),
    (3, "AI in idea generation", "Useful for variations, combinations, and perspectives. It cannot establish truth, desirability, or practicality; people verify and test with customers."),
    (4, "Emotional intelligence (4 abilities)", "Self-awareness, self-management, social awareness, relationship management."),
    (4, "Responding vs. reacting", "Creating a pause between an emotional trigger and an action so you can check facts and choose a useful response."),
    (4, "Trust model", "Trust = Authenticity (genuine, consistent) + Logic (sound reasoning, competence) + Empathy (understands and cares)."),
    (5, "Design thinking", "Iterative, human-centered process: empathize, define, ideate, prototype, test and iterate."),
    (5, "Empathy (vs. sympathy)", "Understanding another person's circumstances, feelings, and needs well enough to see the problem from their view. Not pity, and not rushing to fix it."),
    (5, "Discovery interview", "Talk to people outside friends and family, start broad, ask open questions about real past behavior (“tell me about the last time…”), listen more, probe neutrally."),
    (6, "Team effectiveness (3 outcomes)", "Performance, learning and adaptability, member well-being and growth."),
    (6, "Psychological safety", "A shared belief that the team is safe for interpersonal risk-taking. It does not mean constant agreement, low standards, or no accountability."),
    (6, "Failure vs. mistake", "Failure: an unwanted result from a thoughtful test of genuine uncertainty, which produces information. Mistake: ignores known information, breaks process, or repeats a known error."),
    (6, "Task vs. relationship conflict", "Task conflict debates evidence, assumptions, and choices. Relationship or status conflict attacks people."),
    (6, "Process gains and losses", "Gains: combined knowledge, motivation, error checking. Losses: social loafing, poor coordination, dominant speakers, fear, hidden information, premature consensus."),
    (6, "Team leadership levers", "Design (purpose, membership, roles, resources), launch (shared goals and norms), process management (monitor and intervene). Relaunch when things change."),
    (7, "Customer vs. consumer", "The customer is willing and able to pay; the consumer or user experiences the product. Identify who decides, who pays, who uses."),
    (7, "Segmentation and target market", "Segmentation divides a market by demographics, geography, psychographics, behavior, or need. The target market is the segment you serve first."),
    (7, "Persona", "A fictional ideal customer grounded in research patterns: pains, goals, constraints, workarounds, buying criteria, channels. Details must predict real decisions."),
    (7, "Value proposition", "Why a specific customer chooses this over alternatives. It must resonate, differentiate, and substantiate."),
    (7, "Value proposition template", "For [target], who need [pain or desire], [product] is a [category] that [benefit], because [reason to believe]."),
    (7, "Feature vs. benefit", "A feature is what the product has; a benefit is what the customer gains. Add “which means that…” until you reach a valued result."),
    (7, "Business Model Canvas (9 blocks)", "Customer segments, value proposition, channels, customer relationships, revenue streams, key resources, key activities, key partners, cost structure."),
    (8, "Prototype", "An early representation built to answer a question. Its value is what you learn, not how finished it looks."),
    (8, "Prototype sequence", "Name the riskiest assumption, decide the observable result, build the smallest test, watch customers, record evidence and revise."),
    (8, "Minimum viable product (MVP)", "The smallest usable version that can test whether the venture creates value. Minimum does not mean careless."),
    (8, "Concierge test", "The founder performs the future service by hand to learn whether customers value it before building software."),
    (9, "Rocket Pitch order", "Customer, problem (with evidence), solution, benefit and difference, reason to believe, next action or request."),
    (9, "Value proposition tests (pitch)", "Overt benefit (why care?), dramatic difference (why this?), real reason to believe (why trust it?)."),
    (10, "DiSC", "A shared language for behavioral and communication preferences: D Dominance, I Influencing, S Steady, C Conscientious. Not a measure of ability or worth."),
    (10, "D — Dominance / Driver", "Direct, fast, results-focused. May be impatient. Lead with the result, options, and next action."),
    (10, "I — Influencing / Expressive", "Energetic, persuasive, idea-generating. May skip details. Invite ideas, then confirm details in writing."),
    (10, "S — Steady / Supporter", "Calm, dependable, cooperative. May avoid conflict. Explain changes and allow time to respond."),
    (10, "C — Conscientious / Analytical", "Accurate, logical, quality-focused. May overanalyze. Bring evidence, structure, and thinking time."),
    (10, "Flexing", "Adapting your communication to help the team work, e.g. D vs. C conflict: set a decision deadline and agree on the minimum evidence for a reversible test."),
    (11, "Desirability", "Do real people want or need this? Evidence: stories, repeated pain, workarounds, prototype use, willingness to switch or pay."),
    (11, "Feasibility", "Can this team create and deliver it? Evidence: skills, technology, suppliers, partners, time, quality, operations."),
    (11, "Viability", "Can the model sustain itself? Evidence: price, costs, margins, frequency, acquisition cost, demand, competition."),
    (11, "Alternatives and competition", "Compare direct competitors, indirect alternatives, and the customer's current workaround, including doing nothing."),
    (12, "Primary vs. secondary research", "Primary is collected for this decision; secondary was collected previously by others."),
    (12, "Qualitative vs. quantitative", "Qualitative explores how and why (interviews, focus groups). Quantitative measures how many, how often, how much (surveys, experiments)."),
    (12, "Focus group", "A moderated discussion of 5–8 target customers that uses interaction to develop insight. Exploratory, not proof of market size."),
    (12, "Leading question", "Wording that signals the preferred answer, e.g. “Don't you agree our app makes crafting easier?”"),
    (12, "Convenience sample", "People who are easy to reach, like family. Often biased and may not represent the target customer."),
    (12, "4Ps", "Product, price, place (channel), promotion."),
    (12, "Revenue drivers", "Number and quality of customers, purchase frequency, selling process or channel, and price."),
    (13, "Personal vs. social identity", "Personal: characteristics and experiences that shape self-understanding. Social: group memberships that may affect belonging, status, and opportunity."),
    (13, "Diversity / inclusion / belonging", "Diversity: who is present? Inclusion: whose voice influences the work? Belonging: who feels accepted as a legitimate member?"),
    (13, "Faultlines", "Stable divisions into subgroups (e.g. veterans vs. newcomers) that trap information. Reduce with shared goals, cross-subgroup tasks, transparent decisions."),
    (13, "Inclusive leadership", "Distribute airtime, ask for views before seniors anchor, use clear criteria, explain how input shaped the decision, rotate visibility, never force disclosure."),
    (0, "C-E-A-R", "Concept (name and define), Evidence (specific case fact), Analysis (why it matters), Response and result (observable action and what it improves)."),
]
